<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<worksheet xml:id="writing-assignment-2" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Writing Assignment 2: Systems and Regression</title>

  <introduction>
    <p>
      This assignment covers solving systems of linear equations and building linear regression
      models from data.
    </p>
    <p>
      <ul>
        <li>For Question 1, define your variables, set up your equations, and show your graph.</li>
        <li>For Question 2, round all regression parameters to 3 decimal places.</li>
        <li>Every answer that asks for an interpretation should be written in a complete sentence, in context.</li>
        <li>
          Upload your work to Blackboard. Select Week 2, then select Writing Assignment 2, and
          scroll down to upload a scan of your work. Google Drive has a nice feature for scanning
          documents. Please let me know if you need assistance with this.
        </li>
      </ul>
    </p>
  </introduction>

    <exercise xml:id="wa2-bake-sale">
      <title>A Fundraising Bake Sale</title>
      <introduction>
        <p>
          The math club is running a bake sale to raise money for a conference trip. They sell only
          two items: cookies for $2 each and brownies for $3 each. By the end of the sale, they had
          sold 96 items total and had collected exactly $246 in sales. How many cookies and how many
          brownies did they sell?
        </p>
      </introduction>

      <task workspace="0.25in">
        <statement>
          <p>Define your variables.</p>
          <p>Let <m>x =</m> <fillin characters="40"/></p>
          <p>Let <m>y =</m> <fillin characters="40"/></p>
        </statement>
      </task>

      <task>
        <statement>
          <p>Write two equations that describe this situation.</p>
          <tabular top="minor" left="minor" bottom="minor" right="minor">
            <col halign="left" width="40%" right="minor"/>
            <col halign="left" width="60%"/>
            <row header="yes" bottom="minor">
              <cell>What the equation tracks</cell>
              <cell>Equation</cell>
            </row>
            <row bottom="minor">
              <cell>Total items sold</cell>
              <cell></cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>Total revenue (dollars)</cell>
              <cell></cell>
            </row>
          </tabular>
        </statement>
      </task>
      <task>
        <statement>
          <p>
            Graph the system in Desmos. Sketch both lines on the grid below; choose your own scale,
            label both axes, and mark the intersection point.
          </p>
          <image width="60%">
            <shortdescription>A blank coordinate grid</shortdescription>
            <description>
              <p>
                A blank first-quadrant coordinate grid, twelve units wide and twelve units tall,
                with a horizontal axis labeled x and a vertical axis labeled y.
              </p>
            </description>
            <latex-image>
              \begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.55]
                \draw[gray!30, thin] (0,0) grid (12,12);
                \draw[->, thick] (0,0) -- (12.6,0) node[right] {$x$};
                \draw[->, thick] (0,0) -- (0,12.6) node[above] {$y$};
              \end{tikzpicture}
            </latex-image>
          </image>
        </statement>
      </task>

      <task>
        <statement>
          <p>
            State the coordinates of the intersection point: <m>(x, y) =</m>
            <fillin characters="20"/>
          </p>
        </statement>
      </task>

      <task workspace="1in">
        <statement>
          <p>
            What does this solution mean in the context of the bake sale? Write a complete sentence.
          </p>
        </statement>
      </task>

      <task workspace="1.5in">
        <statement>
          <p>Check your answer by substituting your values back into both equations.</p>
        </statement>
      </task>
    </exercise>


 
    <exercise xml:id="wa2-cricket-regression">
      <title>Cricket Chirps and Temperature</title>
      <introduction>
        <p>
          Crickets are ectotherms, meaning their body temperature (and their chirping speed) is
          driven by the temperature around them. The table below shows real field data: the number
          of chirps a striped ground cricket made in a 15-second span, along with the air
          temperature at the time.<fn>Data source: G. W. Pierce, <pubtitle>The Song of
          Insects</pubtitle>, Harvard University Press.</fn>
        </p>
        <tabular top="minor" left="minor" bottom="minor" right="minor">
          <col halign="center" right="minor"/>
          <col halign="center"/>
          <row header="yes" bottom="minor">
            <cell>Chirps (in 15 seconds)</cell>
            <cell>Temperature (degrees Fahrenheit)</cell>
          </row>
          <row><cell>20</cell><cell>89</cell></row>
          <row><cell>16</cell><cell>72</cell></row>
          <row><cell>20</cell><cell>93</cell></row>
          <row><cell>18</cell><cell>84</cell></row>
          <row><cell>17</cell><cell>81</cell></row>
          <row><cell>16</cell><cell>75</cell></row>
          <row><cell>15</cell><cell>70</cell></row>
          <row><cell>17</cell><cell>82</cell></row>
          <row><cell>15</cell><cell>69</cell></row>
          <row><cell>16</cell><cell>83</cell></row>
          <row><cell>15</cell><cell>80</cell></row>
          <row><cell>17</cell><cell>83</cell></row>
          <row><cell>16</cell><cell>81</cell></row>
          <row><cell>17</cell><cell>84</cell></row>
          <row><cell>14</cell><cell>76</cell></row>
        </tabular>
      </introduction>

      <task workspace="0.75in">
        <statement>
          <p>
            Enter the data into a table in Desmos and adjust your window. Does there appear to be an
            approximately linear structure?
          </p>
        </statement>
      </task>

      <task workspace="0.5in">
        <statement>
          <p>Find the linear regression model for this data.</p>
          <p>
            <ol>
              <li>
                <term>Enter the data.</term> Input the table of values into Desmos, with chirps as
                <m>x_1</m> and temperature as <m>y_1</m>.
              </li>
              <li>
                <term>Find the regression line.</term> Click the style/regression icon next to the
                table, or type <m>y_1 \sim m x_1 + b</m> in the command line below your data matrix.
              </li>
              <li>
                <term>View high precision parameters.</term> Type <m>m</m> or <m>b</m> into an empty
                evaluation line underneath to view extended decimal precision values.
              </li>
            </ol>
          </p>
          <p>
            Regression equation: <fillin characters="40"/> (round the parameters to 3 decimal
            places)
          </p>
        </statement>
      </task>

      <task workspace="1.25in">
        <statement>
          <p>
            Look at your graphed equation along with the data points. Does there seem to be a
            relationship between the number of chirps and the air temperature?
          </p>
        </statement>
      </task>

      <task workspace="1.25in">
        <statement>
          <p>
            A cricket chirps 19 times in a 15-second span. What temperature would you expect it to
            be?
          </p>
        </statement>
      </task>

      <task workspace="1.25in">
        <statement>
          <p>
            Suppose the air temperature is <m>78^{\circ}</m>F. How many chirps would you expect to
            hear in 15 seconds?
          </p>
        </statement>
      </task>

      <task workspace="1.5in">
        <statement>
          <p>
            What is the slope of your model? Write one sentence to describe what it means in the
            context of this problem.
          </p>
        </statement>
      </task>

      <task workspace="1.5in">
        <statement>
          <p>
            What is the vertical intercept of your model? Write one sentence to describe what it
            means in the context of this problem. Does this value make sense here?
          </p>
        </statement>
      </task>

      <task workspace="1.5in">
        <statement>
          <p>
            What is the horizontal intercept of your model? Write one sentence to describe what it
            means in the context of this problem. Does this value make sense here?
          </p>
        </statement>
      </task>
    </exercise>

</worksheet>